{"name":"Cliff Ridge","hex":"#c5ae80","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cliff","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vertical (or nearly vertical) rock face.","A point beyond which something abruptly fails or decreases in value, performance, etc."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Obsolete form of clef."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cliff"},{"term":"ridge","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The back of any animal; especially the upper or projecting part of the back of a quadruped.","Any extended protuberance; a projecting line or strip."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form into a ridge.","To extend in ridges."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ridge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cliff","blocks":[{"text":"In geography and geology, a cliff or bluff or rock face is an area of rock which has a general angle defined by the vertical, or nearly vertical. Cliffs are formed by the processes of weathering and erosion, with the effect of gravity. Cliffs are common on coasts, in mountainous areas, escarpments and along rivers. Cliffs are usually composed of rock that is resistant to weathering and erosion. The sedimentary rocks that are most likely to form cliffs include sandstone, limestone, chalk, and dolomite. Igneous rocks such as granite and basalt also often form cliffs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG/330px-White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cliff"},{"title":"Ridge","blocks":[{"text":"A ridge is a long, narrow, elevated geomorphologic landform, structural feature, or a combination of both separated from the surrounding terrain by steep sides. The sides of a ridge slope away from a narrow top, the crest or ridgecrest, with the terrain dropping down on either side. The crest, if narrow, is also called a ridgeline. Limitations on the dimensions of a ridge are lacking. Its height above the surrounding terrain can vary from less than a meter to hundreds of meters. A ridge can be either depositional, erosional, tectonic, or a combination of these in origin and can consist of either bedrock, loose sediment, lava, or ice depending on its origin. A ridge can occur as either an isolated, independent feature or part of a larger geomorphological and/or structural feature. Frequently, a ridge can be further subdivided into smaller geomorphic or structural elements."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/%E4%B8%AD%E6%A9%AB%E5%85%AC%E8%B7%AF%E7%9C%BA%E6%9C%9B%E6%A2%A8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%8A%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E5%8D%97%E7%A8%9C2.jpg/330px-%E4%B8%AD%E6%A9%AB%E5%85%AC%E8%B7%AF%E7%9C%BA%E6%9C%9B%E6%A2%A8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%8A%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E5%8D%97%E7%A8%9C2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridge"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Trango_Towers_2.jpg/500px-Trango_Towers_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Trango_Towers_2.jpg/500px-Trango_Towers_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Trango Towers in Pakistan. Their vertical faces are the world's tallest cliffs. Trango Tower center; Trango Monk center left; Trango II far left; Great Trango right.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATrango_Towers_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg/500px-Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg/500px-Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Europe's highest cliff, Troll Wall in Norway, a famous BASE jumping location for jumpers from around the world.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATroll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Northern_Areas_40.jpg/500px-Northern_Areas_40.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Northern_Areas_40.jpg/1280px-Northern_Areas_40.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Northern_Areas_40.jpg/500px-Northern_Areas_40.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The far southwestern aspect of Nanga Parbat's Rupal face, highest cliff (rock wall/mountain face) in the world. The steepest part of the face is 2 km to the northeast. Cliffs are very common in areas where there are river banks and oceans.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANorthern_Areas_40.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Vihren_North_face.JPG/500px-Vihren_North_face.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Vihren_North_face.JPG/500px-Vihren_North_face.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vihren's 460 m north face seen from Golemiya Kazan, Pirin Mountain, Bulgaria","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVihren_North_face.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/TalusConesIsfjorden.jpg/500px-TalusConesIsfjorden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/TalusConesIsfjorden.jpg/1280px-TalusConesIsfjorden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/TalusConesIsfjorden.jpg/500px-TalusConesIsfjorden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cliffs along the north shore of Isfjord, Svalbard, Norway.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATalusConesIsfjorden.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Nos_Kaliakra.jpg/500px-Nos_Kaliakra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Nos_Kaliakra.jpg/1280px-Nos_Kaliakra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Nos_Kaliakra.jpg/500px-Nos_Kaliakra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kaliakra cape cliffs, Bulgaria","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANos_Kaliakra.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Matengai_of_Kuniga_Coast_in_Oki_Island_Shimane_pref600.jpg/500px-Matengai_of_Kuniga_Coast_in_Oki_Island_Shimane_pref600.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Matengai_of_Kuniga_Coast_in_Oki_Island_Shimane_pref600.jpg/500px-Matengai_of_Kuniga_Coast_in_Oki_Island_Shimane_pref600.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Matengai in Oki Islands, Japan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMatengai_of_Kuniga_Coast_in_Oki_Island_Shimane_pref600.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Ireland_cliffs_of_moher2.jpg/500px-Ireland_cliffs_of_moher2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Ireland_cliffs_of_moher2.jpg/500px-Ireland_cliffs_of_moher2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Cliffs of Moher in Ireland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIreland_cliffs_of_moher2.jpg"}],"sounds":{"cliff":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d7/En-us-cliff.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/d/d7/En-us-cliff.ogg/En-us-cliff.ogg.mp3"}],"ridge":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/En-us-ridge.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/0/09/En-us-ridge.ogg/En-us-ridge.ogg.mp3"}]}}}